WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.760
Brian Cox. Rockstar physicist who made space&nbsp;
cool for many of us. He is a professor of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:10.980
particle physics at the University of Manchester&nbsp;
and has been the host of many science shows and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:17.400
documentaries including Wonders of the Universe.&nbsp;
Before he specialized in making science easy to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:24.360
understand he wrote one very long and hard to&nbsp;
understand PhD thesis and I've taken one for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:29.280
the team and tried my best to read through&nbsp;
this so that you can understand some of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:33.060
work he did as a student without having&nbsp;
to slog through the whole thing yourself.

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:38.520
Here’s the first page. The thesis is&nbsp;
titled: Double Diffraction Dissociation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:43.020
at Large Momentum Transfer. Don't&nbsp;
worry if that means nothing to you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:47.940
because it meant nothing to me last week but&nbsp;
I think I've got a grip on it now. This was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:53.280
submitted in 1998 at the University&nbsp;
of Manchester to earn Brian Cox the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:58.380
title of Doctor and permit his career to move&nbsp;
forwards onto more research and professorship.

00:00:58.380 --> 00:01:02.760
I learned a lot of interesting particle&nbsp;
physics whilst just trying to understand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:07.260
the thesis introduction. Turns out that&nbsp;
the internal structure of a proton is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:12.300
way more complicated than I knew and&nbsp;
really this thesis is all about trying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:17.100
to gain just a tiny bit more knowledge&nbsp;
about what's going on inside a proton.

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:22.440
In physics we first learn that in an atom&nbsp;
there is a nucleus containing positively&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:29.880
charged protons and neutral neutrons those are&nbsp;
surrounded by negatively charged electrons.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:34.560
Then we might learn that protons&nbsp;
and neutrons are actually made up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:39.600
of smaller particles called quarks but&nbsp;
that is far from where we should stop.

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:46.560
Like charges repel and if electromagnetism was&nbsp;
the only force in charge of the nucleus then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:53.760
it would fall apart so what keeps the nucleus&nbsp;
of an atom intact? There must be some really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:58.920
strong force overcoming the repulsion&nbsp;
that each proton feels from one another.

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:04.140
Protons and neutrons both experience&nbsp;
the strong force but electrons don't.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:09.900
What protons and neutrons have in common&nbsp;
is that they both contain quarks so it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:15.360
seems like quarks have something to do with the&nbsp;
strong force and it turns out that is the case&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:21.000
because quarks can interact with the particle&nbsp;
that carries the strong force, the gluon.

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:26.400
Gluons are massless particles that jump from&nbsp;
quark to quark to mediate the strong force.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:32.820
The study of the strong force and how it works is&nbsp;
called quantum chromodynamics because it relates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:38.340
to the color charge carried by the quarks.&nbsp;
Quarks also come in the flavors up, down,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:45.840
strange, charm, top, bottom and the anti-quarks&nbsp;
of each of those such as anti-up and anti-down.

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:52.500
A quark/anti-quark pair will annihilate if they&nbsp;
collide, the energy could turn into a gluon which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:58.620
can then reappear as another quark/antiquark&nbsp;
pair. We could have an up and an anti-up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.620 --> 00:03:05.160
combine to form a gluon which then becomes a&nbsp;
down and an anti-down and that can continue.

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:11.880
Each gluon can split temporarily into a&nbsp;
quark/anti-quark pair or even two gluons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:18.540
so at any given moment the internal structure of&nbsp;
a proton is actually very complicated. So much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.540 --> 00:03:24.180
so that throughout this thesis instead of saying&nbsp;
quark or gluon when referring to particles inside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:31.200
a proton, the word Parton is often used instead&nbsp;
which means any particle that could be inside.

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:36.060
To find out about this complicated&nbsp;
proton structure, Brian Cox studied&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.060 --> 00:03:40.500
what happens when electrons are fired&nbsp;
at protons in a particle accelerator.

00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:46.560
Chapter 2 of the thesis is all about the H1&nbsp;
detector at the HERA particle accelerator in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:51.540
Hamburg, Germany, which is where Brian&nbsp;
performed these experiments. HERA ran&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.540 --> 00:03:57.540
from 1992 to 2007 and could collide&nbsp;
protons and electrons at high energy.

00:03:57.540 --> 00:04:03.060
To see the unseeable there are three important&nbsp;
parts of particle physics experiments;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.060 --> 00:04:09.300
accelerating particles, smashing them together,&nbsp;
and recording that collision with detectors.

00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:14.700
The particles in Brian’s experiments were&nbsp;
accelerated in the hope of knocking a quark in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.700 --> 00:04:20.760
a proton hard enough that nothing else matters.&nbsp;
If a kid tried to tackle a big rugby player,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:26.580
even with impact, the rugby player would still&nbsp;
have a grip on the ball. But if another rugby&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.580 --> 00:04:31.200
player tackled them, there's a much greater&nbsp;
chance the ball will fly out of their hands.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:36.660
The rugby player might be a proton and the&nbsp;
ball is a quark. In a violent high-energy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.660 --> 00:04:41.220
collision we could knock the quark clear&nbsp;
of the proton and gain new information.

00:04:41.220 --> 00:04:48.720
For charged particles like electrons and protons,&nbsp;
we can accelerate them using electric fields. A&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:53.640
proton in an electric field will feel a force&nbsp;
in the same direction the field is pointing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.640 --> 00:04:59.820
The field might be made by two plates attached to&nbsp;
a battery but instead of one long line of plates,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.820 --> 00:05:06.420
many accelerators are circular and work like&nbsp;
a playground roundabout. If an adult pushes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.420 --> 00:05:12.960
the ride every time it comes past them, it will&nbsp;
spin faster and faster with every push. HERA’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:17.820
synchrotron worked in a similar way using&nbsp;
magnets to keep the particles in a circular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.820 --> 00:05:24.300
path with many points of acceleration. Getting&nbsp;
to that top speed took a few different stages.

00:05:24.300 --> 00:05:29.460
When it comes to detection the most common&nbsp;
technique is to measure ionisation from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.460 --> 00:05:34.500
electrons getting knocked off of atoms. When&nbsp;
a charged particle passes through matter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:40.260
the electric field of the particle knocks&nbsp;
the negatively charged electrons from atoms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.260 --> 00:05:46.500
in the matter leaving positively charged&nbsp;
ions. An external electric field pulls&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.500 --> 00:05:52.740
the electrons and ions even further apart so&nbsp;
they can’t recombine. The detector collects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.740 --> 00:05:58.680
those electrons allowing physicists to measure the&nbsp;
ionisation at each point away from the collision.

00:05:58.680 --> 00:06:04.800
If a runaway car lost its breaks and entered&nbsp;
a cornfield, each plant would slow it down a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:09.600
little. That's what a particle travelling&nbsp;
through the detector is like. A fast and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:14.940
heavy car would take a lot longer to come to&nbsp;
a stop and so would a more energetic particle.&nbsp;

00:06:14.940 --> 00:06:21.240
Magnetic fields also cause charged particles to&nbsp;
move in curved paths with low-energy particles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.240 --> 00:06:28.320
curving more, so detectors can gather information&nbsp;
about the path, charge and energy of a particle.

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:32.880
Chapter 3 of The Thesis discusses more&nbsp;
of the underlying theory motivating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.880 --> 00:06:37.560
the research it's very advanced and&nbsp;
mathematical he goes into more depth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.560 --> 00:06:41.880
about quantum chromodynamics and how&nbsp;
to reconcile it with other theories.

00:06:41.880 --> 00:06:48.420
There is mention of the pomeron, a mysterious&nbsp;
virtual particle. Where the gluon carries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.420 --> 00:06:54.780
the strong force between quarks, the pomeron is&nbsp;
thought to carry the strong force between protons.

00:06:54.780 --> 00:06:58.440
Chapter 4 shows the results&nbsp;
of some experiments and makes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.440 --> 00:07:03.120
mention of observed jets. Jets are&nbsp;
actually pretty cool and weird.

00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:08.160
Turns out that quarks can't exist on their own&nbsp;
once they’ve been knocked out of the proton.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.160 --> 00:07:14.280
This rubber band represents the strong force&nbsp;
connecting two quarks. When the two ends are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.280 --> 00:07:19.380
close there is little force but when the&nbsp;
ends are separated the force increases.

00:07:19.380 --> 00:07:24.600
Think of the rubber band stretching as a&nbsp;
quark and an antiquark moving away from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.600 --> 00:07:30.780
each other after a collision. As the rubber&nbsp;
band becomes tighter, its energy increases&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.780 --> 00:07:37.200
until it snaps. The same concept applies to&nbsp;
quarks and antiquarks. As they move apart,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.200 --> 00:07:43.140
the strong force between them grows stronger until&nbsp;
it becomes too intense for the available space to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.140 --> 00:07:49.140
contain. The energy at this breaking point&nbsp;
transforms into a new quark/antiquark pair.

00:07:49.680 --> 00:07:55.740
Now there are two quark/antiquark pairs, the&nbsp;
original pair still moving apart and the newly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.740 --> 00:08:01.320
created one. When it comes to rubber bands, the&nbsp;
process ends at this point, but with quarks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.320 --> 00:08:06.840
it's just the beginning. The original pair&nbsp;
and the newly created pair continue to move&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.840 --> 00:08:13.560
away from each other and this process repeats,&nbsp;
converting energy into the mass of new particles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.560 --> 00:08:20.160
until the adjacent pairs are no longer&nbsp;
moving away. Then, the quark-antiquark&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.160 --> 00:08:25.800
pairs join to form mesons that travel in a&nbsp;
straight line along the original direction.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.640 --> 00:08:33.480
Mesons are small particles made up of one quark&nbsp;
and one antiquark and examples include the pion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.480 --> 00:08:40.320
and kaon. In this wild process, quarks&nbsp;
and gluons have become a jet of mesons.

00:08:40.320 --> 00:08:47.280
Brian compares the energy distribution of observed&nbsp;
jets to simulations. Brian says he can't draw any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.280 --> 00:08:51.480
statistically significant conclusions&nbsp;
from these first experiments because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.480 --> 00:08:55.560
he encountered many difficulties&nbsp;
including that some parts of jets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.560 --> 00:09:02.160
fell outside of the detector. He also had high&nbsp;
uncertainties in identifying some jets. These&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.160 --> 00:09:05.640
difficulties lead him to think up a&nbsp;
new approach for the next chapters.

00:09:05.640 --> 00:09:11.460
Chapter 5 is where the name of the thesis&nbsp;
comes from. Double Diffraction Dissociation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.460 --> 00:09:17.520
at Large Momentum Transfer. Let’s break&nbsp;
down the title. Dissociation refers to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.520 --> 00:09:22.440
a system of particles breaking apart into&nbsp;
two or more separate final states after a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.440 --> 00:09:28.140
collision. Diffraction means the particles&nbsp;
are scattered. Double means we’ve got two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.140 --> 00:09:34.920
separate systems in the final state. Large&nbsp;
momentum transfer (|t|) just means that a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.920 --> 00:09:39.660
large amount of momentum is transferred from&nbsp;
the incident particles to the scattered ones.

00:09:39.660 --> 00:09:45.480
The example studied throughout this work is a&nbsp;
high-energy photon (the basic unit of light)&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.480 --> 00:09:52.740
interacting with a proton and producing two&nbsp;
jets, call them X and Y, in the final state.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.740 --> 00:09:59.820
X is the backward jet from the photon side&nbsp;
and Y is the forward Jet from the proton side.

00:09:59.820 --> 00:10:06.480
The new focus here and in chapter 6 is in&nbsp;
measuring a Double dissociation Differential&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.480 --> 00:10:11.880
Cross Section. A cross-section is a measure&nbsp;
of the size of the interaction area between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.880 --> 00:10:17.340
two particles in a collision. It’s like&nbsp;
a probability that the interaction will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.340 --> 00:10:23.280
occur. Brian measured the probability of the&nbsp;
double dissociation interaction as a function&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.280 --> 00:10:29.280
of energy and the angle of particles relative&nbsp;
to the beam axis, to see if the interaction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.280 --> 00:10:34.920
is more or less likely as the energy of the&nbsp;
particles involved in the collision increase.

00:10:34.920 --> 00:10:40.860
In the summary, we can see that Ryan Cox&nbsp;
has compared the cross-section results to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.860 --> 00:10:46.680
perturbative Quantum chromodynamics model&nbsp;
and found that it was consistent with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.680 --> 00:10:52.380
colorless object, the theorized pomeron,&nbsp;
being exchanged in the interaction. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.380 --> 00:10:57.420
results were not conclusive enough to say&nbsp;
for sure but that finding was still one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.420 --> 00:11:03.180
the main contributions of the thesis. Another&nbsp;
contribution was having measured the double&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.180 --> 00:11:08.580
dissociation differential cross-section in&nbsp;
this specific energy range for the first time.

00:11:09.600 --> 00:11:16.620
Since this thesis was published in 1998, particle&nbsp;
physics has made a lot of progress but the ideas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.620 --> 00:11:22.140
Brian studied are still relevant. Efforts were&nbsp;
made to combine the quark models described here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.140 --> 00:11:28.260
with the standard model of particle physics.&nbsp;
The standard model does not include a pomeron,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.260 --> 00:11:34.140
so with our current understanding, whilst it was&nbsp;
a useful mathematical object, it doesnt seem the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.140 --> 00:11:40.020
pomeron is a real particle, in the sense that&nbsp;
it isnt detectable. Interestingly however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.020 --> 00:11:44.340
since modern string theory arose&nbsp;
from trying to explain the results&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.340 --> 00:11:49.140
of scattering experiments involving&nbsp;
particles with 2 or more quarks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.140 --> 00:11:56.400
the mathematics describing pomerons has a new use&nbsp;
in string theory and has lived to see another day.

00:11:56.400 --> 00:12:02.520
You might associate Brian Cox more with&nbsp;
the study of space than particles but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.520 --> 00:12:09.300
astrophysics and cosmology stand hand in hand with&nbsp;
particle physics. Many of the particles studied&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.300 --> 00:12:15.300
in colliders were only present in the universe&nbsp;
during the earliest moments after the big bang.

00:12:15.300 --> 00:12:20.400
At that time the universe was very hot and&nbsp;
particles were moving extremely quickly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.400 --> 00:12:26.280
with a lot of energy. This thesis studied&nbsp;
particles with high energy and experiments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.280 --> 00:12:31.560
like these are recreating the conditions&nbsp;
of the early universe. Through this we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.560 --> 00:12:37.080
can understand how matter came together and&nbsp;
why the universe contains anything at all.

00:12:37.080 --> 00:12:42.240
Particle physics is a way to perform&nbsp;
experiments to understand the wonders&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.240 --> 00:12:47.040
of the universe rather than relying&nbsp;
on observations of space alone.

00:12:47.040 --> 00:12:50.940
Thank you for watching this video&nbsp;
and thanks to my Patreon supporters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.940 --> 00:12:55.980
for making it possible. A special shout out&nbsp;
to today's Patreon cat of the day Stanley.

